• 13) How do indexes affect query performance, and when can they hurt?
"Indexes improve query performance by reducing the need for full table scans and enabling faster lookups, joins, sorting, and aggregations. They are especially useful for columns used in WHERE, JOIN, ORDER BY, and GROUP BY clauses. However, indexes can hurt performance when there are too many of them because they increase storage requirements and add overhead to INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE operations. Therefore, indexes should be created based on query patterns and validated using execution plans like EXPLAIN ANALYZE."
"In one project, a customer search API was taking around 5 seconds. After analyzing the execution plan, we found a full table scan on a table containing millions of records. We added a composite index on (customer_id, status) and reduced query execution time to less than 100 milliseconds. However, we also reviewed existing indexes and removed several unused ones because they were increasing insert latency."
