7.	How does CopyOnWriteArrayList work internally? When would you prefer it?
8.	CopyOnWriteArrayList is a thread-safe collection where every write operation such as add, remove, or update creates a new copy of the underlying array. Readers access an immutable snapshot, so read operations do not require synchronization and can run concurrently without blocking.
9.	Its iterators are snapshot-based, which means they never throw ConcurrentModificationException. The tradeoff is that write operations are expensive because the entire array is copied on each modification.
10.	I prefer CopyOnWriteArrayList in read-heavy and write-light scenarios such as listener registries, configuration caches, and observer lists. I avoid it when the application performs frequent updates because array copying can cause significant CPU and memory overhead.
11.	Follow-up Interview Question
12.	Q: Why doesn't CopyOnWriteArrayList throw   ConcurrentModificationException?
Answer: Because the iterator works on a snapshot of the array taken at iterator creation time. Any later modifications create a new array, leaving the iterator's snapshot unchanged.
7. What is the difference between fail-fast and fail-safe iterators?
A fail-fast iterator traverses the original collection and throws a ConcurrentModificationException if it detects a structural modification after the iterator is created. Collections like ArrayList, HashMap, and HashSet use fail-fast iterators by tracking modifications through an internal modCount.
A fail-safe iterator operates on a snapshot or a concurrent view of the collection, so modifications during iteration do not cause exceptions. Examples include CopyOnWriteArrayList and ConcurrentHashMap iterators. The trade-off is additional memory overhead or relaxed consistency.
In real-world concurrent applications, I prefer fail-safe or concurrent collections when reads and writes happen simultaneously, whereas fail-fast collections are suitable when modifications during iteration indicate a programming error that should be detected early.
Q: Can a fail-fast iterator guarantee that it will always throw ConcurrentModificationException?
Answer: No. Fail-fast behavior is best-effort, not guaranteed. In multi-threaded environments, concurrent modifications may not always be detected immediately, so ConcurrentModificationException should be used as a bug-detection mechanism, not as a synchronization mechanism.
