9. How does TreeMap maintain ordering? Can we provide custom sorting logic?
TreeMap maintains ordering using a self-balancing Red-Black Tree. By default, it sorts keys according to their natural ordering through the Comparable interface. All operations such as insert, search, and delete take O(log n) time because the tree remains balanced through rotations and recoloring.
Yes, we can provide custom sorting logic by supplying a Comparator during TreeMap creation. In that case, TreeMap uses the comparator for all key comparisons instead of natural ordering.
One important distinction is that TreeMap determines key uniqueness using compareTo() or Comparator.compare(), whereas HashMap relies on equals() and hashCode(). I typically use TreeMap when I need sorted data, range queries, or navigational methods like higherKey(), lowerKey(), ceilingKey(), and floorKey().
