11. What is the difference between @Transactional at class level vs method level?
@Transactional at the class level applies the same transactional behavior to all public methods in the class, which helps reduce repetition and maintain consistency. @Transactional at the method level provides fine-grained control and can override class-level settings.
In real projects, I typically place @Transactional at the service class level for common transactional behavior and override specific methods with settings such as readOnly = true, different propagation levels, or isolation levels when required. Method-level annotations always take precedence over class-level annotations. Also, since Spring uses proxy-based AOP, internal method calls within the same class do not trigger transactional interception. This is an important consideration when designing service layers.
